<!--
THESIS: The page tells the one moment the product is built around: you double-click a Markdown file. It shows what a Mac gives you today (a wall of punctuation, or an invitation to set up a workspace) and then what Skål gives you (the file, as a document). It refuses the feature grid and the diff; the positioning is viewer-first and the default handler, and source preservation is a trust line low on the page.
OWN-WORLD: Blekk's pasteboard (#E7E2D8) as the desk, warm paper (#FAF7F1) for the objects on it, ink #211E1A, rust headings, the app's magenta on the primary action and on the one selected Finder row. Captures are trimmed to the window edge and cast the page's own shadow, so every object on the desk shares one shadow. Source Serif 4 for headings and prose; the system sans for anything that is UI (file rows, window chrome, buttons). No mono anywhere, since nothing on the page is source.
STORY: "That is exactly what happens to me." The visitor recognises the moment, sees the better outcome, learns it also fixes Finder previews, and joins the beta.
FIRST VIEWPORT: Small wordmark row. The headline "The .md handler you actually want." A one-line dek. Then a Finder-style list of three Markdown files on paper with Skål's own document icons, "Field notes.md" selected, captioned "You double-click one of these." The raw-text panel and the Skål capture below both show that same file, so the double-click completes. Then Finder, the default handler, the three views, the trust line, the Not list and the beta. The two "what you get today" panels begin below the fold line.
FORM: sequential story; direction 4 of 5. No seed key: the user asked for more examples, which pins the directions and replaces the roll. Code-led, both themes.
FINISH: unreviewed and undocumented is unfinished; this build ends with the finish review, the verdict, and DESIGN.md
-->
