  <section class="sec">
    <h2>What Skål gives you.</h2>
    <p>The file, as a document. It opens alone, with no folder, no sidebar, and no chrome it hasn't earned. Read it, edit it, close it. It is still the same <code>.md</code> file, in the same place.</p>
    <figure class="one">
      <img src="/shots/skal/landing/shot-live.webp" width="2560" height="1720" alt="Skål with every panel off: Field notes, September set as prose on warm paper, with only Code, Live and Split in the status bar." loading="eager">
      <figcaption>No account. No library. No new format between you and your words.<small>Files, Outline and the toolbar are three switches you turn on, not off. Add a heading and it appears in Outline; press Check Markdown and Skål reports what it found, with a fix you can review before anything changes.</small></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn" href="/skal/b/go/">Join the TestFlight beta</a>
      <span class="fact">Requires macOS 26 or later.</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec two">
    <figure>
      <img src="/shots/skal/landing/shot-quicklook-light.webp" width="1620" height="1286" alt="A Finder Quick Look panel showing Field notes.md rendered as prose, with Live and Code toggles and an Open with Skål button." loading="lazy">
    </figure>
    <div>
      <h2>Often you don't even need to open it.</h2>
      <p>Skål adds Markdown previews and thumbnails to Finder. Press the space bar on a <code>.md</code> file and read it rendered, on your chosen paper, without opening anything. Quick Look is read only and never fetches a remote image.</p>
      <p><b>Most encounters with a Markdown file aren't editing sessions.</b> Skål is built in that order: a reader that happens to edit.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <h2>When you do want to write.</h2>
    <p>One file, three views. Read and edit rendered prose in <b>Live</b>. Work on the exact source in <b>Code</b>. Keep both in view with <b>Split</b>, scrolling together.</p>
    <figure class="one">
      <img src="/shots/skal/landing/shot-split.webp" width="2560" height="1720" alt="Skål in Split view: the Markdown source of Field notes on the left, the same document rendered as prose on the right, with Code, Live, Split and Linked in the status bar." loading="lazy">
    </figure>
    <ul class="views">
      <li><b>Live</b><span>Clean prose at rest. Type Markdown and it becomes formatting; undo and the characters come back.</span></li>
      <li><b>Code</b><span>The file as it is on disk, with line numbers and wrapped lines.</span></li>
      <li><b>Split</b><span>Both, side by side and linked. Double-click the divider for an equal split.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="sec two">
    <figure class="narrow">
      <img src="/shots/skal/landing/shot-get-info.webp" width="530" height="1026" alt="The Finder Get Info window for Field notes.md, with Open with set to Skål as the default and a Change All button." loading="lazy">
    </figure>
    <div>
      <h2>Make it the default.</h2>
      <p>Get Info on any Markdown file, choose Skål under Open with, and press Change All. From then on, a double-click means Skål, and a Markdown file on your Mac is just a document again.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="trust">
      <h2>It never rewrites your file.</h2>
      <div>
        <p>Some Markdown editors rebuild the whole file from their own model when you save, so lines you never touched come back reformatted. Skål saves what it opened, plus what you changed. Open a file and save it without editing and it comes back byte for byte.</p>
        <p>You will only ever notice this if you keep your notes in git or share them with other tools. If you do, you will notice it every day.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <h2>Not in Skål.</h2>
    <ul class="nots">
      <li><b>No account.</b><span>Nothing to create, nothing to unlock. Plain files in the folders you already keep them in.</span></li>
      <li><b>No sync service.</b><span>iCloud Drive, Dropbox and git already do that, and the file is just a file.</span></li>
      <li><b>No AI of our own.</b><span>The suggestions are bounded to what is in the document.</span></li>
      <li><b>No plugins, no themes.</b><span>You choose a paper in Settings. That is the extent of it.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="close" id="beta">
    <h2>Skål is in beta.</h2>
    <p>Build 0.1 has been submitted to TestFlight and is waiting on Apple's review. It will be a small paid Mac app when it ships; the beta is free, and the most useful thing you can send back is a file it got wrong.</p>
